// @id 01M6S1M10KZREMXQBRVNDYH6B5 model=cloze
q: DC stands for ==Direct Current== while AC for ==Alternating Current==.

// @id 01M6S1M10KHVCY93ZFENJ6XMN2 model=basic-example
q: The formula for calculating the AC current as specific time is defined as..
a: $$A(t) = A_{max} * \sin{2\pift}$$

// @id 01M6XY255GMYD9QVS9ARM2PRPW model=cloze
q: ==Time Period (T)== is the length of time in seconds that a waveform takes to complete one full cycle. For sine waves it is also called ==Periodic Time==, for square waves ==Pulse Width==.

// @id 01M6XY255GSCKZPSHCTG7KKN4V model=basic-example
q: Name the common types of periodic waveforms.
a: - Sine wave
- Complex wave
- Triangular wave
- Square wave
[image:media/periodic-waveforms.webp]

// @id 01M6XY255GNK1CKRFR51AEEDFR model=cloze
q: ==Frequency (ƒ)== is the number of complete cycles a waveform completes in one second, measured in ==Hertz (Hz)==.
e: Frequency is the reciprocal of the time period:
$$f = \frac{1}{T}$$

// @id 01M6S1M10KXW6NXX3M9WQ0HED9 model=basic-example
q: The amplitude of a waveform is defined as..
a: The greatest value of either voltage or current that the waveform reaches during each half cycle measured from the zero baseline.
[image:media/sinusoidal-waveform.webp]

// @id 01M6XY255GG9GNAYHQ37XFFXER model=basic-example
q: The peak-to-peak voltage ($V_{p-p}$) of a waveform is defined as..
a: The difference between the maximum positive peak ($+V_{max}$) and the minimum negative peak ($-V_{max}$) during one complete cycle. For a sine wave $V_{p-p} = 2V_{max}$.
[image:media/sinusoidal-waveform.webp]

// @id 01M6XY255GEQDT802BVCJXN5G9 model=cloze
q: ==RMS (Root Mean Square) voltage== is the effective value of an AC voltage, equal to the DC voltage that would deliver the same power (heating effect) to a resistive load.
e: $$V_{RMS} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{T}\int_0^T v(t)^2\,dt}$$
Also called the effective value, $V_{eff}$.

// @id 01M6XY255GP2KQPAZ3Q5CBXPJF model=basic-example
q: The RMS voltage of a *pure sinusoidal waveform* is calculated as..
a: $$V_{RMS} = \frac{V_{pk}}{\sqrt{2}} \approx 0.7071 \cdot V_{pk}$$
e: Only valid for pure sinusoids. For non-sinusoidal waveforms use the graphical (mid-ordinate) method.

// @id 01M6S1M10KMY3GG2BZMX9A8TK6 model=cloze
q: ==Form Factor (FF)== is the ratio between the RMS value and the average value defining the shape or “peakiness” of the AC waveform.
e: $$\text{FF}=\frac{\text{R.M.S value}}{\text{Average values}}$$
Note that a higher form factor will indicate a more “peaky” waveform, while a lower value would indicate a flatter wave shape.

// @id 01M6S1M10K0X72EW8YRPKE7Y10 model=cloze
q: ==Crest Factor (CF)== of a sinusoidal waveform is the ratio between its maximum or peak value and its RMS value defining the maximum voltage of the waveform.
e: $$\text{CF} = \frac{\text{Peak value}}{\text{R.M.S value}}$$

// @id 01M6S1M10KDG2V9S64HGTN800A model=basic-example
q: The amount of EMF induced into a coil cutting the magnetic lines of force is determined by what three factors?
a: - The **speed** at which the coil rotates inside the magnetic field
- The **strength** of the magnetic field
- The **length** of the coil or conductor passing through the magnetic field

// @id 01M6XY255G50BEEGHRR801V7YK model=cloze
q: A ==radian (rad)== is the angle at the centre of a circle subtended by an arc whose length equals the radius.
e: A full circle (one full cycle of a waveform) is $2\pi$ rad $= 360^\circ$, so $1\,\text{rad} = \frac{360^\circ}{2\pi} \approx 57.3^\circ$.
[image:media/radians.webp]

// @id 01M6S1M10KSNQKZJ42C8FXS5F5 model=cloze
q: ==Phase Difference / Phase Shift / Phase Delay==, defines the difference in time between two sinusoidal waveforms of the same frequency.
e: The phase difference of two waveforms indicates how much one leads or lags behind the other.
[image:media/phase-difference.webp]

// @id 01M6XY255GGDEGAQDV2SK9S7DT model=basic-example
q: A phasor diagram is..
a: A graphical representation of the magnitude and phase relationship between AC quantities of the same frequency. Each quantity is drawn as a vector rotating anticlockwise at angular velocity $\omega$, "frozen" at some instant, relative to a reference phasor along the positive x-axis.
[image:media/phasor-diagram.webp]

// @id 01M6XY255GCA75C9G6EQ6W2VPR model=cloze
q: ==Impedance (Z)== is the total opposition a circuit offers to the flow of alternating current, measured in ==ohms (Ω)==.
e: It is the vector sum of resistance (R) and reactance (X). For a pure resistor $Z = R$.

// @id 01M6XY255GP85Z1C4JJS5XWZGT model=basic-example
q: The impedance of a circuit is calculated as..
a: $$Z = \frac{V}{I} = R + jX$$
$$|Z| = \sqrt{R^2 + X^2}$$
e: For a series RLC circuit $X = X_L - X_C$, so $|Z| = \sqrt{R^2 + (X_L - X_C)^2}$.

// @id 01M6XY255GHASV9CZ5WG1EXKEW model=basic-example
q: The average power consumed by a pure resistance in an AC circuit is..
a: $$P = V \cdot I = I^2 R = \frac{V^2}{R}$$
e: V and I are RMS values. These are the same as the DC formulas because voltage and current are in phase.

// @id 01M6YJJG15Z8SSG0XGKNFGRJRF model=cloze
q: ==Reactance (X)== is the opposition of an inductor or capacitor to alternating current, measured in ==ohms (Ω)==. Unlike resistance, it depends on ==frequency==.
e: It is the imaginary part of impedance, $Z = R + jX$, where $X = X_L - X_C$.

// @id 01M6YJJG15VYVG0AXGS11Q919Y model=basic-example
q: The inductive reactance $X_L$ in terms of frequency $f$ is calculated as..
a: $$X_L = 2\pi f L$$
- $X_L$ – inductive reactance [Ω]
- $f$ – frequency [Hz]
- $L$ – inductance [H]

// @id 01M6YJJG15PPQ3S4GTNM2R95Y0 model=basic-example
q: The inductive reactance $X_L$ in terms of angular frequency $\omega$ is calculated as..
a: $$X_L = \omega L$$
- $X_L$ – inductive reactance [Ω]
- $\omega = 2\pi f$ – angular frequency [rad/s]
- $L$ – inductance [H]

// @id 01M6YJJG150D0V974HFMPSC8KM model=cloze
q: The mnemonic for the phase relationship in an AC inductance is ==ELI==: the voltage (E) in an inductor (L) ==leads== the current (I) by 90°.

// @id 01M6YJJG157GT5P0HYSCCDX4Y7 model=basic-example
q: How do inductive reactance $X_L$ and current change as frequency increases?
a: $X_L$ increases linearly with frequency ($X_L \propto f$), while the current decreases.
[image:media/current-reactance-frequency.webp]

// @id 01M6YJJG15D6T6AZSTNA1NQEQ6 model=basic-example
q: How does an inductor behave at $f = 0$ (DC) and at $f \to \infty$?
a: - $f = 0$: $X_L = 0$, acts as a short circuit, maximum current
- $f \to \infty$: $X_L = \infty$, acts as an open circuit, $I = 0$
[image:media/inductance-frequency.webp]

// @id 01M6YJJG151MY8JQKP3S3BVSMP model=basic-example
q: The impedance of a series RL circuit is calculated as..
a: $$Z = R + jX_L$$
$$|Z| = \sqrt{R^2 + X_L^2}$$
e: Phase angle $\phi = \tan^{-1}\frac{X_L}{R}$, with the current lagging the voltage.

// @id 01M6YJJG159B8RNY2H9WN0KJ3R model=cloze
q: The mnemonic for the phase relationship in an AC capacitance is ==ICE==: the current (I) in a capacitor (C) ==leads== the voltage (E) by 90°.

// @id 01M6YJJG15ATFV86K8T6A6X6NB model=basic-example
q: The capacitive reactance $X_C$ in terms of frequency $f$ is calculated as..
a: $$X_C = \frac{1}{2\pi f C}$$
- $X_C$ – capacitive reactance [Ω]
- $f$ – frequency [Hz]
- $C$ – capacitance [F]

// @id 01M6YJJG15KT8W3D0Y040ASDQB model=basic-example
q: The capacitive reactance $X_C$ in terms of angular frequency $\omega$ is calculated as..
a: $$X_C = \frac{1}{\omega C}$$
- $X_C$ – capacitive reactance [Ω]
- $\omega = 2\pi f$ – angular frequency [rad/s]
- $C$ – capacitance [F]

// @id 01M6YJJG15J3HPN69RWSJ5XEHQ model=basic-example
q: How do capacitive reactance $X_C$ and current change as frequency increases?
a: $X_C$ decreases, being inversely proportional to frequency ($X_C \propto \frac{1}{f}$), while the current increases.
[image:media/current-capacitive-reactance-frequency.webp]

// @id 01M6YJJG15T86P144TQ3C4MK93 model=basic-example
q: How does a capacitor behave at $f = 0$ (DC) and at $f \to \infty$?
a: - $f = 0$: $X_C = \infty$, acts as an open circuit, $I = 0$ (blocks DC)
- $f \to \infty$: $X_C = 0$, acts as a short circuit, maximum current
[image:media/capacitance-frequency.webp]

// @id 01M6YJJG156ZRMBGDGD8MGD1AK model=basic-example
q: The impedance of a series RC circuit is calculated as..
a: $$Z = R - jX_C$$
$$|Z| = \sqrt{R^2 + X_C^2}$$
e: Phase angle $\phi = \tan^{-1}\frac{X_C}{R}$, with the current leading the voltage.

// @id 01M6YJJG15WFT52T3F42SNEFYV model=basic-example
q: In a series RLC circuit, what is the phase of the voltage across each element relative to the common current?
a: - $V_R$ is in phase with $I$
- $V_L$ leads $I$ by 90°
- $V_C$ lags $I$ by 90°
So $V_L$ and $V_C$ are 180° out of phase with each other.
[image:media/series-rlc-phase-relationship.webp]
[image:media/rlc-voltage-diagrams.webp]

// @id 01M6YJJG150GW7N1GGYNV8CY95 model=basic-example
q: The impedance of a series RLC circuit is calculated as..
a: $$Z = R + j(X_L - X_C)$$
$$|Z| = \sqrt{R^2 + (X_L - X_C)^2}$$
e: If $X_L > X_C$ the circuit is inductive (current lags the voltage); if $X_C > X_L$ it is capacitive (current leads the voltage).
